::: {.callout-note}
## Choosing the decay rate

A decay of 1.0 (the default) gives equal weight to all batches. Values closer to 0 give more weight
to recent data. A decay of 0.5 means each batch halves the weight of all previous batches. Choose
based on how quickly you expect the underlying relationship to change.
:::

## Monitoring and re-estimation

### Smoothing history

Each time you call `solve()`, the streaming GAM records a snapshot of the model state. You can
inspect this history to monitor how the model evolves over time.

::: {#fbdc8ab7 .cell execution_count=8}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Build a longer history
rng = np.random.default_rng(42)
sgam = wk.StreamingGAM("y ~ s(x)")

for batch_idx in range(10):
    n_batch = 50
    x_batch = rng.uniform(0, 2 * np.pi, n_batch)
    y_batch = np.sin(x_batch) + rng.normal(0, 0.3, n_batch)
    sgam.partial_fit({"x": x_batch, "y": y_batch})
    sgam.solve()

# Inspect the history
history = sgam.smoothing_history()
print(f"Number of snapshots: {len(history)}")
for snap in history[:3]:
    print(f"  n_obs={snap.n_obs}, batches={snap.n_batches}, edf={snap.edf_total:.1f}")
```

